text:
Check: compile and run
hello.cpp:
#include &lt;iostream&gt;
int main()
{
  std::cout &lt;&lt; "Hello from C++!" &lt;&lt; std::endl;
  return 0;
}
g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -o hello hello.cpp   # compile
./hello                                    # run: Hello from C++!

-std=c++17 selects the C++ standard; -o hello the name of the executable.
-Wall turns on the compiler warnings: always use it. Today you will see why (an empty method that should return a value compiles, with a warning only).
-O2 (optimization) will be important for the simulations, not today.

If ./hello prints the line, the environment is ready.

Example 0: vectors in C
C++_EXAMPLES/N0_from_c_to_cpp/ex0_basic_c_types.c (excerpt):
double d, e, f;   /* d, e, f are instances of the type double */
d = 1.2;
e = 2.3;
f = d + e;        /* for double, + is built in */

double va[3]={1.2, 1.3, -1.0};
double vb[3]={0.2, 0.1, 0.4};
double vc[3];
/* vc = va + vb is not possible: an explicit loop is needed */
for (i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++)
  {
    vc[i] = va[i] + vb[i];
  }
/* ...and another loop (3 times!) to print va, vb and vc */
For double we write f = d + e, for vectors we have to spell out every component, every time.

Example 1: better, with functions
ex1_vec_with_funcs.c:
void sumvec(double va[3], double vb[3], double vc[3]);
void printvec(double vec[3], char* txt);

int main(void)
{
  double va[3]={1.2, 1.3, -1.0}, vb[3]={0.2, 0.1, 0.4}, vc[3];
  sumvec(va, vb, vc);        /* vc = va + vb */
  printvec(va, "va");
  printvec(vb, "vb");
  printvec(vc, "vc=va+vb");
}

Less repetition, but sumvec(va, vb, vc) is not vc = va + vb.
Data (double[3]) and functions acting on them live separately: nothing tells the compiler that sumvec belongs to “vectors”.
What we want: a type vector that carries its data and its operations.

Example 2: a first C++ class
ex2_a_cpp_class.hpp (without the #includes):
class myclass
{
1  int a;              // private members: by default everything in a class is private
  std::string txt;
  void show_private(void) const { std::cout &lt;&lt; "no!" &lt;&lt; std::endl; }   // private method
2public:               // from here on: the interface
  void show(void) const
    {
      show_private(); // a method can use the private members
      std::cout &lt;&lt; txt &lt;&lt; " " &lt;&lt; a &lt;&lt; std::endl;
    }
3  myclass()           // constructor: called when the object is created
    { txt = "Hallo World"; a = 2; std::cout &lt;&lt; "Object created" &lt;&lt; std::endl; }
4  ~myclass()          // destructor: called when the object is destroyed
    { std::cout &lt;&lt; "Object destroyed" &lt;&lt; std::endl; }
};

1

In a struct the default is the opposite: everything public.

2

main can call C.show(), but not C.show_private() nor read C.a: try it, the compiler refuses.

3

Same name as the class, no return type.

4

~ + class name, no arguments: here it only prints, later it will free resources.

C struct vs C++ class


C
struct vec {
  double v[3];
};

struct vec sumvec(struct vec a,
                  struct vec b);

struct vec a, b, c;
a.v[0] = 1.0;     /* direct access */
c = sumvec(a, b);

C++
class pvector {
  double v[3];     // private
public:
  void set(int i, double x);
  pvector operator+(const pvector&) const;
  // ...
};

pvector a, b, c;
a.set(0, 1.0);    // through the interface
c = a + b;        // operator overloading


Both define a type, and a, b, c are instances of it (objects).
The class also contains the operations, and decides what the user can touch.
a.v[0] = 1.0 on a pvector does not compile: v is private. If tomorrow we change how the data are stored, the code that uses pvector does not change.

Example N1: a first vector class
C++_EXAMPLES/N1_a_first_vec_class/myvec.hpp:
class myvec
{
  int v[3];  // private members
  int len;
public:
  myvec()  { len=3; }        // constructor
  ~myvec() { }               // destructor: nothing to do

  void set(int i, int val) { v[i] = val; }   // assign the (i+1)-th element
1  int get(int i) const       { return v[i]; }
  int length(void) const     { return len; }
};

1

const method: it does not change the state of the object. Every method receives implicitly the object it is called on (*this); const makes it read-only.


myvec v;                                          // instantiation
for (int i=0; i &lt; v.length(); i++) v.set(i, 0);   // initialize the elements
v.set(1, 1);  v.set(2, -2);
std::cout &lt;&lt; "v[1]=" &lt;&lt; v.get(1) &lt;&lt; " v[2]=" &lt;&lt; v.get(2) &lt;&lt; std::endl;   // v[1]=1 v[2]=-2

Example N2: inheritance
C++_EXAMPLES/N2_a_derived_vec_class/vecder.hpp:
#include "../N1_a_first_vec_class/myvec.hpp"
1class vecder: public myvec    // vecder inherits everything from myvec
{
public:
  void show(void) const       // ...and adds one public method
    {
      std::cout &lt;&lt; "(";
      for (int i = 0; i &lt; length(); ++i)
        {
2          std::cout &lt;&lt; get(i);           // length() and get() are public methods of the base class
          if (i &lt; length()-1) std::cout &lt;&lt; ",";
          else                std::cout &lt;&lt; ")" &lt;&lt; std::endl;
        }
    }
};

1

public inheritance: the public interface of myvec stays public in vecder.

2

Writing v[i] here would not compile: v is private in myvec, not even a derived class sees it. The way out (protected) is for next lab.


vecder vd;
for (int i=0; i &lt; vd.length(); i++) vd.set(i, i);   // set() comes from myvec
vd.show();                                         // (0,1,2)

The task
Write from scratch an efficient class for vectors, with methods and overloaded operators: LABWORK_2026-27/pvector.hpp (the class) and pvector.cpp (a main to test it).
The header begins with a TODO list: [*] = to do today.
// [*] standard constructor/destructor
// [*] constructor (with overloading-&gt;initializer_list)
// [*] show()
// [*] overloading of = assignment
// [*] overloading of + operator for addition of vectors
// [*] overloading of - operator for substraction of vectors
// [*] sum() method to sum two vectors
// [*] get( ) (get i-th element)
// [*] set( , ) (set i-th element)
// [ ] overloading of += and -= operators with vectors          &lt;- next labs
// [ ] overloading of * operator for scalar product ...
The skeleton already contains all the signatures, and operator= is implemented: your job is to fill the empty bodies.

The skeleton: NT and ntype
1#define NT 3                // STRATEGIES TO MAKE CLASS GENERIC
2//constexpr int NT=3;

//typedef float ntype;      // as in C
3using ntype=double;

class pvector
{
  ntype v[NT];              // private member: the NT components
public:
  ...
};

1

A macro: the preprocessor replaces the text NT with 3 before compiling. No type, no scope.

2

constexpr: a typed constant that the compiler can compute at compile time — so it can size an array. In C++ it is the preferred way.

3

using defines an alias of a type, exactly like typedef. Change this single line to float and the whole class works in single precision.



Dimension and type are fixed in one place each: with NT=2 you get vectors in the plane, with no other change.
In a few labs templates will make both parameters of the class itself: pvector&lt;double,3&gt;, pvector&lt;float,2&gt;.

The initializer_list constructor
#include &lt;initializer_list&gt;          // add it among the includes

pvector(std::initializer_list&lt;ntype&gt; L)   // overloading of the constructor
  {
    int c=0;
1    for (ntype el: L)                // range-based for: el takes, one after
      {                              //   the other, the values in the braces
        // copy el in v[c], if c &lt; NT (extra values are ignored)
        c++;
      }
    // set to zero the components from c to NT-1, if any
  }

1

for (ntype el: L) reads “for each el in L”: no index, no size to know. It works on every container (std::vector, arrays, …).



std::initializer_list&lt;ntype&gt; is the type of {1.0, 2.0, 3.0}: the compiler builds it and passes it to the constructor.
The compiler chooses the constructor from the arguments: pvector P; calls the void one, pvector V = {1,2,3}; this one. This is overloading.

show(): default arguments and const
// Note this method does not change data of object hence it is declared "const"
void show(std::string s="") const    // default argument
  {
    ...   // print s, then (v[0],v[1],v[2])
  }
V.show("V=");   // V=(1,2,3)
V.show();       // (1,2,3)      s takes the default value ""

Default argument: if the caller omits it, s is "".
const method: it only reads the object. The compiler checks it (assigning v[i] inside show() is an error), and only const methods can be called on a const object:
const pvector PC = {0.2, 0.3, 0.4};
PC.show("PC=");   // compiles only because show() is const
Use std::cout with a loop over the NT components, and a comma between them.

The main: pvector.cpp
The skeleton only declares three vectors. Test every method, for example:
#include "./pvector.hpp"
int main(void)
{
  pvector A = {1.0, 2.0, 3.0}, B = {0.5, -1.0, 2.0};
  pvector C, D = {4.0, 5.0};   // C=(0,0,0), D=(4,5,0)

  A.show("A=");
  D.show("D=");
  C = A + B;      C.show("A+B=");
  C = A - B;      C.show("A-B=");
  C = A.sum(B);   C.show("A.sum(B)=");

  B.set(0, 10.0);
  std::cout &lt;&lt; "B(0)=" &lt;&lt; B.get(0) &lt;&lt; "\n";

  pvector E, F;
  E = F = A;      // chained assignment: operator= returns pvector&
  E.show("E=");

  const pvector PC = {0.2, 0.3, 0.4};
  PC.show("PC=");   // ok only because show() is const
  return 0;
}
